## Finding 1 (blocking) — the absent half of the present-vs-absent distinction has no negative test

`internal/config/schema.go:480` `restorePresentEmptyMaps` is the new discriminator
between "closed map member present but empty" and "closed map member absent". Its
whole contract is conditional on `if !sourceField.IsValid() { continue }`
(`schema.go:499`). The present-and-empty half is now well covered. The absent half
— the refusing half — is covered by no test.

Narrowing mutant, applied to `/tmp/axmut4` and run as
`go test ./internal/config -count=1`: initialize a nil map member whenever the
destination field is a map, before the source-presence check.

```go
sourceField := source.MapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(name).Convert(source.Type().Key()))
destinationField := destination.Field(index)
if destinationField.Kind() == reflect.Map && destinationField.IsNil() {
        destinationField.Set(reflect.MakeMap(destinationField.Type()))
}
if !sourceField.IsValid() {
        continue
}
```

Result: **SURVIVED — the full `internal/config` package stays green**, including
the new refusal-site inventory gate.

Behavior under that mutant, probed through `Load` only (documents complete except
for the one omitted member):

| Document | Candidate tree | Under mutant |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[[directory_installations]]` without `extensions` | refused | **accepted** |
| `[[directory_enrichment_profiles]]` without `extensions` | refused | **accepted** |
| `[[directory_peer_disclosure]]` without `extensions` | refused | **accepted** |
| `[[terminal.backend_config]]` without `settings` | refused | **accepted** |

Why the suite misses it: the four `... required member` cases in
`refusal_test.go:153-190` each omit *six or seven* members at once (only
`installation_id` / `profile_id` / `host_id` / `backend_id` are supplied), so they
still refuse on an earlier disjunct and cannot notice the `Extensions` /`Settings`
disjunct being neutralized. This is the shape the DoD names: a gate that admits
what it must reject, with a green suite.

Related, same root cause — four narrowing mutants dropping only the closed-map
disjunct from each presence check (`validation.go:109`, `:114`, `:119`, `:133`)
**all SURVIVED**. These four are in fact fail-closed, subsumed downstream by
`validateExtensions(nil)` returning `ErrConfigValidation` (`validation.go:735-737`)
and by `entry.Settings == nil` in `validateTerminal` (`validation.go:722`) — but
the DoD requires a survivor to be documented as subsumed with the subsuming check
named, and none is.

`README.md` now states that "Required closed map members preserve the distinction
between absence and an explicit empty object". That claim is true of the code and
carried by evidence on only one side of the distinction.

Requested: one negative case per member (four total) driving `Load` with a
document that is otherwise complete and omits exactly the closed map member,
asserting the `... required member` clause. That single addition kills the
`restorePresentEmptyMaps` mutant above and makes the four disjunct mutants
redundant; alternatively name `validateExtensions(nil)` and `validation.go:722` as
the subsuming checks for the disjuncts in-code.

## Finding 2 — the extension canonical byte bound tests the constant, not the declared bound

SPEC.md SS6 declares the extension object canonical bound as 65,536 bytes (lines
350 and 2496 of the pinned document). `internal/config/validation.go:16` holds it
as `maxConfigExtensionBytes`.

`schema_test.go:555-568` builds both the at-limit and the past-limit fixture from
`maxConfigExtensionBytes` itself, and its own self-check
(`if len(encodedJSON) != maxConfigExtensionBytes`) compares against the same
symbol. The fixture therefore scales with whatever the constant says.

Mutant `const maxConfigExtensionBytes = 655_360`: **SURVIVED**, package green. A
64 KiB-declared bound silently becoming 640 KiB ships.

This is the only declared bound in the suite tested this way. Every neighbouring
bound uses a spec literal — 64/65 for `host_name`, 1024/1025 for `endpoint`,
4096/4097 for one `ssh_arg`, 32_766/32_767 for a path, 253/254 for the extension
key via composed labels — and all of those kill their widening mutants.

Requested: derive the at-limit and past-limit payloads from the literal `65_536`
(or from a pinned spec-derived source) rather than from the implementation
constant, so the declared bound is what is proven.

